mudlib: Fading Summer
---------------------
This directory contains documentation for all aspects of the mudlib.
Organization is as follows:

/doc
 |
 |- administration
 |- coding
 |- commands
 |- driver
 |- objects
 |- specs
 |- theme

administration: This directory contains documentation designed to
       make the job of the administrators easier.  It is filled
       with suggestions and guidelines for administration of the
       mudlib, expansion of the mudlib, and maintenance of the
       mudlib.
 
coding: This directory contains tutorials, scenarios, and helpful
       documents to help both the expert and the novice LPC coder
       make the most of his or her experience with this mudlib.

commands: This directory contains detailed help on specific commands
       as well as tutorials and guidelines for their proper usage.

driver: This directory contains the documentation tree from MudOS.
       This documentation covers efuns, concepts behind the driver
       itself, and other driver-related information.  Most people
       will not ever need to look here, but if you want to learn
       what efun does what, or you have found yourself at a dead
       end trying to solve a problem, this may be the documentation
       you are searching for.

objects: This directory contains information on each of the parent
       objects (inheritable objects) in the mudlib.  This is where
       you will find detailed information on just what functionality
       you gain from inheritting a standard object, such as a weapon
       or an npc or even a room.

specs: This directory contains specifications for various parts
       of the mudlib.  Documents describing object security,
       specifications on the user object, etc. can be found here.

theme: This directory contains information relating to the theme of
       this mud.  This mudlib was designed for a mud called Estilore,
       so this directory will mainly apply to those who code and play
       there.


If you are a completely new coder, or you want to learn new concepts
to improve your coding ability, you should read the help files
available under the 'coding' directory.  If you want to familiarize
yourself with our theme, naturally you should study the 'theme'
directory well.  If you want to learn about our command system here,
please check out the 'commands' directory.  Note that these files
can be browsed and viewed individually, or they can be accessed via
our help system, which is the recommended method.  Our help system
involves the 'help' and 'man' commands.

Please note the difference in the 'help' and the 'man' commands.  For
short and direct help, use the help command.  However, if you wish to
read a more in depth and descriptive text on a subject, use the man
command.  In short, 'help' for a quick refresh, and 'man' for the last
resort... the manual =).  (Shouldn't that be the first resort?)
